      "source": [
        "# Loan Approval Prediction — Model Training\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1. Project Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "**Problem statement.** A lender receives loan applications and must decide whether each one should be *Approved* or *Rejected*. Given an applicant's financial profile, can we build a model that predicts that decision?\n",
        "\n",
        "**Objective.** Train and compare two models, **Logistic Regression** and **Random Forest**, on the structured applicant data, evaluate them honestly on unseen data, and check that any high accuracy is legitimate (no leakage, no majority-class shortcut).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Machine learning task.** Supervised **binary classification**.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why classification?** The target `Approval` has exactly two discrete outcomes (`Rejected`, `Approved`). We predict a category (or the probability of a category), not a continuous number. That is why we use *Logistic Regression* (a linear **classifier**) and not Linear Regression.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Target variable.** `Approval` → `Rejected = 0`, `Approved = 1`. The classes are imbalanced (≈ 83.6 % Rejected, ≈ 16.4 % Approved).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Models compared.**\n",
        "1. Logistic Regression (linear, interpretable baseline)\n",
        "2. Random Forest Classifier (non-linear ensemble of decision trees)\n",
        "\n",
      ]
    },
    {
