        "id": "e85f177f"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Discussion: trade-offs between the metrics\n",
        "\n",
        "*(Figures refer to the executed run above; the code cells always show the live numbers.)*\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Random Forest scored higher on all four metrics** (accuracy 99.58 %, F1 98.74 %) than Logistic Regression (accuracy 92.62 %, F1 77.54 %).\n",
        "* **Accuracy flatters Logistic Regression.** Its 92.62 % is only about 9 points above the 83.60 % obtained by always predicting *Rejected*. Its recall of 77.64 % means roughly one in five truly approved applicants was missed, and its precision of 77.44 % means roughly one in four *Approved* predictions was wrong. The Approved-class metrics tell the real story.\n",
        "* **Precision vs recall is a genuine trade-off.** In cross-validation (Section 7), `class_weight=\"balanced\"` raised Logistic Regression's recall to about 95 % but cut its precision to about 65 %, giving a lower F1 than the default. That is why the default variant was kept.\n",
        "* **Random Forest reached 100 % training accuracy** but 99.58 % on the test set, and its cross-validated accuracy was about 99.5 %. The small train/test gap and the consistent CV scores suggest the score is not a one-off, although the forest clearly fits the training data very closely.\n",
        "* **Interpretability vs performance.** Logistic Regression yields simple coefficients, the Random Forest gives up that simplicity for much better performance on this dataset. Which is preferable would depend on the use case, so neither is called universally \"best\" from accuracy alone."
      ]
    },
