      "source": [
        "### Verifying that the accuracy is legitimate\n",
        "\n",
        "| Check | Result |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| Training data ≠ test data | Split once with `train_test_split`; asserted that no row index is shared |\n",
        "| Preprocessing fitted on training data only | Scaling and encoding live inside pipelines; scaler mean asserted equal to the *training* mean |\n",
        "| No target leakage | `Approval` and `Text` are not in the feature matrix (asserted) |\n",
        "| No duplicate leakage | 0 test rows share a feature combination with a training row; 0 exact duplicates in the dataset |\n",
        "| No test-set tuning | Variant selection and the grid search used cross-validation on the training set only |\n",
        "| No label manipulation | Labels were only mapped `Rejected → 0`, `Approved → 1` |\n",
        "| Not just predicting the majority class | The always-*Rejected* baseline scores 83.60 % accuracy with 0 % recall; the models find most *Approved* cases |\n",
        "| Training accuracy not reported as test accuracy | Shown separately, labelled \"not the result\" |\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why is Random Forest so high?** The following are plausible explanations, *not* proven facts. (1) The dataset appears to be synthetic and may have been generated from a fairly clean rule, which a tree ensemble can learn almost exactly. (2) `Employment_Status` is extremely informative here: in the training data no unemployed applicant was approved. (3) Logistic Regression can only draw a linear boundary, so it cannot capture interactions or thresholds that trees can. Such a score should not be expected on real, noisier lending data."
      ]
