      "source": [
        "## 17. Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "**What was trained.** A Logistic Regression and a Random Forest classifier predicting loan `Approval` from `Income`, `Credit_Score`, `Loan_Amount`, `DTI_Ratio` and `Employment_Status`, using scikit-learn pipelines so preprocessing was fitted on training data only. The 20 % test set was stratified and used once for final evaluation.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Metrics used.** Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score, confusion matrices and classification reports, plus a majority-class baseline for context.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Effect of class imbalance.** With about 84 % *Rejected*, an always-*Rejected* model already reaches 83.60 % accuracy, so accuracy alone is misleading. Approved-class precision, recall and F1 were essential. `class_weight=\"balanced\"` was compared against the default and chosen by cross-validated F1 rather than assumed to be better.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Measured results (test set, threshold 0.5).**\n",
        "* Logistic Regression: accuracy 92.62 %, precision 77.44 %, recall 77.64 %, F1 77.54 %.\n",
        "* Random Forest: accuracy 99.58 %, precision 98.24 %, recall 99.24 %, F1 98.74 %.\n",
        "\n",
        "Both models exceeded the 90 % accuracy goal, with the leakage and baseline checks in Section 16 supporting that the result is legitimate. On every measured metric, Random Forest performed better on this dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "**What was learned.**\n",
        "* Pipelines and stratified cross-validation prevent leakage and give trustworthy estimates.\n",
        "* Accuracy must be read against the majority-class baseline; Logistic Regression's accuracy looked good while its Approved-class recall was only about 78 %.\n",
        "* Feature importance and coefficients describe what the models use, not causes. The near-perfect Random Forest score, the strong `Employment_Status` pattern and the earlier EDA suggest synthetic data, so the results should not be generalised to real lending.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Future work.**\n",
        "* Explore the `Text` column (e.g. TF-IDF) and see whether it adds signal.\n",
        "* Try other models such as gradient boosting and compare them fairly.\n",
        "* Add more evaluation views (ROC-AUC, precision-recall curves, calibration) and choose a threshold on validation data.\n",
        "* Test on more realistic, noisier data.\n",
        "* Deployment is planned as a **separate later phase**; it is not part of this notebook."
      ]
